.TH git-sync "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Git Command"
.SH NAME
git-sync \- pull and then push the current branch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git sync
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBgit sync\fR command brings the current branch up to date with its remote branch and publishes the
local commits: \fBgit pull\fR, then \fBgit push\fR, each printed as \fBExecuting: git ...\fR before it runs.
.PP
It stops when the pull fails, for example on a merge conflict, so nothing is pushed then. Both commands
use the upstream branch and push target of the current branch, as they do on their own.
.PP
Git runs it as a subcommand because the executable \fBgit-sync\fR is in the \fBPATH\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.B git sync
